meta {
  name: Email Output
  type: http
  seq: 15
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/mailer-campaigns/{{campaignId}}/email
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "recipients": ["print@vendor.invalid"]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 202 Accepted", function () {
    // 202, not 200: the send is a queued Inngest job. A 200 would promise the
    // mail had left, which this endpoint cannot know.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(202);
  });

  test("reports how many recipients were queued", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().queued).to.equal(1);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /platform/mailer-campaigns/:campaignId/email — re-send the output

  Mails the completion notice for an **imported** campaign: a time-limited
  presigned download link for the print file, plus a link back to this campaign
  page.

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `platform:mailers:write`
  - **Data scope:** none (`@SkipTenant()`, `@SkipBranch()`)

  ## Why this exists when the commit already sends one

  The commit emits the same event as its last step, so the happy path needs no
  call here at all. This is the recovery: the printer never got it, the address
  was wrong, or `settings.outputRecipients` was empty at commit time — as it is
  in this chain, which is why the request passes a list explicitly.

  Both paths emit **one** event type into the same worker function, so a re-send
  is not a second implementation of the mail.

  ## Request
  ```json
  { "recipients": ["print@vendor.example"] }
  ```

  ⚠ The address this request actually sends is `print@vendor.invalid` — a
  reserved TLD that can never be delivered — because with `RESEND_API_KEY` set
  locally this queues a **real** outbound send, exactly as
  `Platform Agencies › Onboard Agency` does with `ada@acme.test`. Keep any
  address you add here on a reserved domain.

  `recipients` is optional: omit it and the campaign's stored
  `settings.outputRecipients` are used. At most 20 addresses, each validated as
  an email.

  ## Response `202`
  ```json
  { "queued": 1 }
  ```

  Accepted, not sent. The worker signs a fresh download URL per recipient at
  send time — **never** at commit time and never stored on the campaign, because
  a presigned URL is a bearer capability with an expiry, and one minted hours
  earlier would already be dead.

  ## Duplicate sends
  The provider idempotency key is
  `mailer-campaign:<campaignId>:<commitAttempt>:<recipient>`, so calling this
  twice with the same address for the same run delivers one email, while adding
  a **new** address to the list still delivers to that address. That grain is
  deliberate — a coarser key would swallow exactly the recovery this endpoint
  is for.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — no recipients anywhere (empty stored list and none
    passed), a malformed address, or more than 20.
  - `401` / `403` — missing token, or missing `platform:mailers:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such campaign.
  - `409 Conflict` — the campaign has not imported, so there is no output file
    to link to.
}
